refentry.refsect1:
&reftitle.returnvalues;

 Returns an associative array containing unpacked elements of binary
 string, &return.falseforfailure;.

&reftitle.changelog;







     &Version;
     &Description;





     7.2.0

      float and double types supports both Big Endian and Little Endian.




     7.1.0

      The optional offset has been added.

  &reftitle.examples;



    unpack example


<?php
$binarydata = "\x04\x00\xa0\x00";
$array = unpack("cchars/nint", $binarydata);
print_r($array);
?>


    &example.outputs;


Array
(
    [chars] => 4
    [int] => 160
)








    unpack example with a repeater


<?php
$binarydata = "\x04\x00\xa0\x00";
$array = unpack("c2chars/nint", $binarydata);
print_r($array);
?>


     &example.outputs;


Array
(
    [chars1] => 4
    [chars2] => 0
    [int] => 40960
)

&reftitle.notes;
Note that PHP internally stores integral values as signed. If you unpack a large unsigned long and it is of the same size as PHP internally stored values the result will be a negative number even though unsigned unpacking was specified.
If you do not name an element, numeric indices starting from 1 are used. Be aware that if you have more than one unnamed element, some data is overwritten because the numbering restarts from 1 for each element.
unpack example with unnamed keys
<?php $binarydata = "\x32\x42\x00\xa0"; $array = unpack("c2/n", $binarydata); var_dump($array); ?>
&example.outputs;
array(2) { [1]=> int(160) [2]=> int(66) }
Note that the first value from the c specifier is overwritten by the first value from the n specifier.
